course: dictionary
lesson: 2306
title: Criticise: saying what is wrong, and what is good
level: primary-5
stars: 3
explain: To criticise means to say what you think is bad or wrong about someone or something. It can also mean to judge the good and bad points of a book, film or piece of work. «Criticise the idea, never the person» is a useful rule: you can say an idea is weak, but you should not attack the person who had it.
explain: Read the words around criticise to find its meaning. «An attack ad criticises the other candidate instead of praising your own» tells you that criticise is the opposite of praise. Praise means saying good things; criticise means pointing out faults.
explain: Criticism is the noun for this action. Constructive criticism means helpful comments that show how to improve, and it helps you learn. Be criticised for means you are judged as wrong for something you did.
fact: To criticise means to say what is bad or wrong, or to judge the good and bad points of a book, film or work.
fact: The US spelling is criticize.
fact: «In 1517 Martin Luther, a German monk, criticised the Church for selling forgiveness.»
fact: «Crunch» is when game teams work very long hours before a deadline, and it is widely criticised for harming health.
fact: Constructive criticism is helpful feedback that shows how to make something better.
exercise: «Criticise the idea, never the person.» What should you criticise? | *The idea | The person | The colour of their pencil
exercise: «An attack ad criticises the other candidate instead of praising your own.» What is the opposite of criticise here? | Copy | Forget | *Praise | Argue
exercise: Mia's teacher gives her constructive criticism on her story. What does that mean? | Rude comments that make her cry | A test with a mark out of ten | *Helpful comments that show how to improve | Her teacher reads the story aloud
test: «Crunch is widely criticised for harming health.» What does criticised mean here? | Praised for being fun | Played loudly | *Said to be bad or wrong | Put in a box
test: Which is the best way to criticise a friend's drawing? | Tear it up | Say "it's rubbish" and laugh | Say nothing at all, ever | *Say which part works, which part could be better, and why
exam: In the sentence «In 1517 Martin Luther criticised the Church for selling forgiveness», what does criticised mean? | *Said that something was wrong | Agreed completely | Sold forgiveness | Printed books
